FILED s543p (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27, CODE 0ab08642 = MAIN-READY-111, v2-16300) by the Rosetta census (docs/rosetta-plan.md step 1; #2104).

THE SHAPE (neutral reproducer, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    package Num;
    use overload 'int' => sub { "INT" }, 'abs' => sub { "ABS" }, '0+' => sub { 42 }, fallback => 1;
    sub new { bless {}, shift }
    package main;
    my $x = Num->new;
    print int($x), "\n";                 # perl INT      PCL 42
    print abs($x), "\n";                 # perl ABS      PCL ABS   (#1005's dispatch)
    use Math::BigInt;
    my $b = Math::BigInt->new("12345678901234567890123");
    my $i = int($b);
    print ref($i) || 'plain', "\n";      # perl Math::BigInt          PCL plain
    print int($b) / 7, "\n";             # perl 1763668414462081127160  PCL 1.76366841446208e+21

WHERE: cl/pcl-runtime.lisp `p-int` (~6757) has no overload arm; the comment above `%p-abs-overload` says "`int' is deliberately absent: perl DOES autogenerate it from `0+' and PCL's numification is already that answer" -- true for a class WITHOUT an `int` handler, wrong for one that HAS one (Math::BigInt / BigFloat / BigRat all do: `int` returns an object).  #1005 (completed) is the closed task whose fix did not reach this shape.

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): the `Num` rows -- `abs` dispatches and `int` does not, with both handlers present, so it is the missing `int` arm and not the overload table.  In the program, replacing `int($n / $b)` by `$n / $b` for an object operand makes it identical to perl.

THE PROGRAM: 0351-Digital-root converts a Math::BigInt to a base by repeated `%` and `int($n / $base)`: after the first `int` the value is a plain number, the later divisions are doubles, and the digits (so the persistence count) of the big number come out wrong.

=== s543p (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27, CODE 496df8b1 = MAIN-READY-113) === Rosetta 1122-RSA-code holds this cause too (`use bigint`; base conversion by repeated `int($n / $radix)` and a square-and-multiply loop halving with `int`): after the first `int` the value is a plain number and the digits of the 43-digit numbers are wrong.  Dropping the two `int` calls (a bigint division is already an integer division) makes 1122 identical to perl -- its only cause.  Reproducer: the one above (`int($b) / 7`).
LABEL: silent (ordinary: Rosetta 0351, 1122)

